<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The SEMICOLON guard on a parameter-entity reference, which no committed fixture
  pinned before this one.

  `xml/xsd-parameter-entity-refs` pins the OTHER guard — it forbids a `references`
  edge to `zz` for the value form `% zz '&#60;'`, so a rule that accepted any
  `%...;` would be caught there. The semicolon test had no pin at all, and that is
  the guard a tree-walk removes.

  ## Why the guard exists at all

  `grammars/tree-sitter-xml/xml/src/grammar.json` defines

      PEReference = SEQ[STRING "%", SYMBOL Name, STRING ";"]

  so a well-formed `PEReference` always ends in `;`. But `<!ELEMENT %pe EMPTY>` —
  a parameter entity reference missing its semicolon, which is what a hand-edited or
  truncated DTD looks like — still yields a `PEReference` node whose only named
  child is a `Name`, and that node's text is `%pe` with no `;` in it.

  Measured by `validation/m14/fixtures/xml/pereference-shape-probe.mts` on that exact
  shape: reading the `Name` child publishes a use of `pe`, while the shipped
  predicate publishes nothing. The document never wrote `%pe;`, so the edge would be
  invented. This fixture is what makes that difference observable.

  ## The four rows

    1. `%declared;` as a direct child of the internal subset -> PUBLISHED, owned by
       the DOCTYPE, and it RESOLVES because row 0 declares `declared`. The positive
       case: without it, a rule that published nothing at all would satisfy every
       forbid below.
    2. `<!ELEMENT %unterminated EMPTY>` — the reference form with the semicolon
       MISSING -> NOT published, even though `unterminated` IS declared by row 0b.
       So the forbid cannot be satisfied by a rule that simply refuses undeclared
       names, and cannot be satisfied by a rule that ignores this position.
    3. `% zz '&#60;` — the parameter-entity VALUE form, a DECLARATION of `zz` and not
       a use of it -> NOT published. Already pinned in xml/xsd-parameter-entity-refs
       for the replacement-text position; repeated here in the bare internal-subset
       position because that is a different parse shape — children `ERROR,Name,ERROR`
       against a clean `Name` — and the probe measured the two shapes reaching the
       same guard by different routes.

  ## Why the two references use DIFFERENT names

  The obvious design puts both references on one name, so that only the semicolon
  separates them. It does not work here: edge identity in this store is
  source|type|target, so a broken rule publishing `references root -> pe` twice
  produces ONE edge and the forbid row cannot see the second one. Two names make the
  defect a NEW edge, which is observable. The trade is that the fixture no longer
  proves the semicolon is the only difference — the probe's per-shape table does
  that, and it is committed beside this file.
-->
<!DOCTYPE root [
  <!ENTITY % declared "declared-value">
  <!ENTITY % unterminated "unterminated-value">
  %declared;
  <!ELEMENT %unterminated EMPTY>
  % zz '&#60;'
]>
<root>text</root>
